# 11.1_NATIVE_ARCHITECTURE_BLUEPRINT_WORKING

All repo paths in the numbered control stack are repo-root-relative.

## Status

This addendum contains admitted alpha-stage clarification and current implementation-truth correction for `docs/11_NATIVE_ARCHITECTURE_BLUEPRINT_CORE.md`.

The core remains the target code-structure authority. This addendum does not replace its package, component-form, ownership, seam, bridge/shell, persistence, test, reservation, allocation, traceability, anti-mess or code-acceptance laws. It clarifies how those laws apply to the present incomplete alpha repository and supersedes only the specific status and evidence statements identified below.

## 11.1 Authority, acceptance evidence and external lineage

The header reference in the core to `var/audit/ledgers/native_blueprint/acceptance_receipt.json` is stale: that path is not present in the current repository. It must not be cited as current acceptance evidence.

The blueprint remains normative because it is routed under `docs/01_NORMATIVE_CORE.md`, retained in the accepted numbered corpus and semantically reconciled through the current user-authorised programme. The normative baseline manifest will record that authority. The missing historical receipt neither deletes the blueprint nor proves its current acceptance.

The core statement that source-provenance language need not appear inside the normative body means that vendor citations do not need to interrupt every architecture proposition. It does not waive lineage. Each adopted external principle must have a dated source, project-specific adaptation, rejected interpretations and relationship to the affected normative proposition in the programme external-source decisions and baseline crosswalk.

`CURRENT_PROJECT_STATE.md`, `NVDA10_CURRENT_STATE_ARCHITECTURE_MAP.md`, historical roadmaps, closure receipts and prior plans are evidence or planning surfaces. They cannot override this normative pair or create current implementation status. Where they contain stale branch, licence, source-maturity, proof-closure or row-status claims, those claims remain historical evidence until separately reconciled.

## 11.2 Target architecture versus current alpha implementation

The eighteen architecture surfaces in core section 5 remain the canonical top-down order for architecture review, implementation alignment and traceability.

They are not a claim that the current repository executes eighteen runtime steps end to end. In particular:

- surfaces 5.1 to 5.15 describe input, preparation, decision, downstream-state and review responsibilities in their lawful architectural order;
- surface 5.16 persistence is a cross-cutting state boundary used by multiple earlier and later surfaces;
- surface 5.17 testing and proof is a cross-cutting acceptance boundary;
- surface 5.18 vocabulary governance is a cross-cutting language-admission boundary;
- playbook, coefficient, persistence, proof and vocabulary surfaces may be consumed at several points and must not be misread as one literal serial call chain.

The current repository is an alpha implementation with placeholders, incomplete composition and no paper-data run. It is not production-ready, paper-trading validated, durable across all material state, operationally recovered, capacity-tested or deployment-defined.

For core line 15, `production code` means active native source intended to become the maintained system rather than archived donor or test-only material. It does not mean that the system has reached production maturity or operational deployment.

A target architecture proposition may be valid before its implementation exists. Conversely, the presence of code, tests or receipts does not prove that the target architecture is implemented, correctly composed or adequately proved.

## 11.3 Current implementation-truth admission

The table below is the current normative implementation-truth status for the eighteen surfaces. It records whether an active native implementation form exists; it is not a pass/fail assessment and it does not waive defects.

| Surface | Structural status | Current implementation-truth status | Target area | Honest current alpha statement |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 5.1 External source interfaces and API ingress | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | `src/nvda_desk/adapters/ingress` plus the `src/nvda_desk/api` composition boundary | Typed ingress and local API seams exist; API composition still owns unrelated mapping and state responsibilities. |
| 5.2 Raw capture and source temporal law | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | `src/nvda_desk/ingress`, temporal domain code and temporal contracts | Raw bundle and source-time handling exist locally; authoritative durable raw capture and complete replay evidence do not. |
| 5.3 Raw signal families | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | `src/nvda_desk/upstream` | Typed raw/minimally-derived signal boundaries exist locally; extension and full upstream composition remain to be proved. |
| 5.4 Financial and event calendar architecture | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | `src/nvda_desk/calendar` and `src/nvda_desk/reference` | Calendar/reference behaviour and bounded SQL-backed state exist; provider substitution and complete runtime composition remain incomplete. |
| 5.5 Prepared ingress and runtime context preparation | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | `src/nvda_desk/preparation` | Prepared-context contracts and services exist; all required input families are not yet composed into one governed run. |
