| 5.18 Vocabulary governance interface | `Locked` | `thin_seam` | normative vocabulary documents, schema, builder, scripts and generated projection | Governance tooling exists; semantic registry/projection parity and clean isolated proof remain incomplete. |

No surface is currently admitted as `implemented` under the core definition. A later status upgrade requires exact native code, composition and proof evidence for the claim being made.

The current status changes for 5.10 to 5.14 admit that substantive but incomplete native alpha behaviour exists. They do not approve the current algorithms as correct, authorise live or paper operation, establish durable state, or remove the `Provisional` structural status from 5.10, 5.12, 5.13 or 5.14. Gate B must still inspect each boundary semantically, and Gates C/D must prove and align the implementation.

## 11.4 Modularity and future-change law

Future-proofing means that a known changing implementation can be added, replaced or removed through one explicit owned boundary without rewriting unrelated domains. It does not mean predicting every future requirement or creating a generic plugin framework before a real boundary exists.

The following volatility points are already known and must remain replaceable:

- external providers and source formats through adapters plus versioned typed input contracts;
- raw input families through owned typed family contracts and a controlled registration/configuration boundary;
- playbook and strategy definitions through a playbook contract and registry, with orchestration consuming the contract rather than strategy internals;
- coefficient and modifier policies through governed configuration and bounded policy contracts;
- risk policies through owned policy/service boundaries rather than module-global mutable state;
- persistence technology through repository/store contracts and an explicit composition root;
- broker or simulated-execution implementations through downstream contracts and adapters;
- review/export representations through read-only projections over preserved authoritative evidence.

Adding a fifth or tenth playbook must not require rewriting the runtime orchestrator, API composition, persistence implementations, unrelated playbooks or downstream state owners. A new playbook may require a new implementation, semantic vocabulary admission, registry/configuration entry and its own proof, but the stable playbook consumer contract must remain unchanged unless the contract itself is lawfully versioned.

A common envelope or base interface must preserve strong typed payload contracts. Extensibility must not be achieved by moving domain meaning into ungoverned dictionaries, opaque extension blobs, string dispatch or broad `Any` values.

No microservice, message broker, dynamic plugin loader, service mesh, cloud deployment surface or distributed state mechanism is required by this alpha architecture. The modular monolith remains the target form unless an actual ownership, scaling, isolation or deployment requirement later justifies separation.

## 11.5 Component, ownership and composition boundaries

The core component-form table remains binding. The following clarifications apply:

- an API route or entry point owns transport handling and composition only; it must not become the semantic owner of domain mapping, persistence truth or identifier creation;
- an orchestrator owns order and coordination only; it consumes owned stage contracts and must not contain hidden stage, strategy, risk or capital policy;
- a registry owns lawful lookup and selection metadata only; it must not become an untyped global service locator;
- a repository or store owns state access for one named state domain; it must not own the domain decision that creates the state;
- a contract or packet owns shape and invariants only; it must not manufacture upstream authority or execute domain logic;
- a service may implement one bounded domain action but must receive infrastructure dependencies through explicit construction or a lawful composition root.

Semantic ownership, authorised writing, coordination, reading and review are separate responsibilities under `docs/07_RUNTIME_OWNERSHIP_AND_CONSUMER_LEDGER_CORE.md` and its working addendum. File placement or a class name does not by itself prove ownership.

The current `src/nvda_desk/api/app.py` composition concentration, direct/private state access and process-global risk state are implementation gaps, not accepted blueprint patterns.

## 11.6 Persistence and alpha technology direction

The current declared dependency set supports SQLAlchemy and PostgreSQL-capable drivers. Current source also contains bounded SQLite-backed persistence. This is present technical direction, not a production database decision.

At alpha stage:

- in-memory repositories are lawful only when explicitly labelled non-durable and kept behind the same bounded contract expected of a later persistent implementation;
- SQL-backed stores may prove a bounded persistence seam without establishing that every material state domain is durable;
- each material state domain must name its semantic owner, authorised writer, readers, current implementation, current durability status and minimum replay evidence during Gate B;
- production retention, backup, recovery objectives, high availability, capacity and deployment constraints remain outside current authority.

Persistence technology must not leak into domain algorithms or require unrelated domain changes when a repository implementation changes.

## 11.7 Proof, status and historical evidence

A closure receipt, roadmap row, test manifest, proof block, passing test count or current-state map is evidence only. It cannot set normative architecture status or prove that a target surface is correctly composed.

`bounded_closed_current` and similar historical ledger phrases are bounded programme states, not implementation-truth values in the blueprint status model.

The current architecture map remains useful historical evidence for locating files and prior receipts, but its May 2026 hashes and status claims are stale after subsequent normative and source changes. It must not be used as the current implementation-truth ledger.

The programme implementation-truth ledger under `project_control/normative_authority_top_down_alignment/` is an evidence surface derived from this normative status table. The normative table governs; the ledger records detailed current evidence and later Gate B updates.

## 11.8 Deterministic non-AI scope

The present NVDA runtime is deterministic code and algorithms. No language-model, agent, tool-calling, handoff, prompt, model-provider or agent-tracing runtime is authorised or implemented.

OpenAI agent architecture guidance therefore does not define current runtime structure. Importing agent, handoff, prompt or model abstractions into the deterministic domain would be speculative and is prohibited.

If a future user-approved model or agent capability is introduced, it requires a new normative addendum that defines its bounded purpose, typed inputs/outputs, model/provider configuration authority, deterministic fallback or stand-down behaviour, tracing/privacy boundary, evaluation proof and prohibition from silently acquiring trading, risk, capital or persistence authority.

## 11.9 Blueprint freeze criteria

The blueprint portion of the normative baseline may freeze when:

1. the eighteen target surfaces retain one coherent review order and their cross-cutting roles are explicit;
2. each surface has one structural status and one truthful current implementation-truth status;
3. active alpha behaviour is not labelled `reserved` or `future_only`;
4. target owners, target areas, component forms, lawful seams, persistence boundaries and test families are explicit enough to begin the top-down Gate B walk without guessing;
5. known provisional and reserved allocations remain visible;
6. stale receipts and historical ledgers are not represented as current authority;
7. adopted external principles and rejected interpretations are recorded outside the normative body with stable lineage;
8. no production, paper-data, durability, replay or end-to-end claim exceeds direct evidence.

Freeze means this becomes the controlled alpha benchmark. It does not mean all code satisfies the benchmark, all target modules exist, all implementation details are locked, or later numbered addenda are prohibited.

## 11.10 Accepted ownership-module architecture

The user accepted the Atlas ownership allocation on 2026-07-25 after an extended architecture, dependency and source-boundary review. The eighteen architecture surfaces remain the normative responsibility map. The following sixteen ownership modules are the accepted target code-capability map inside the governed modular monolith.

| ID | Accepted target module | Owns | Must not own | Current alpha disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `M00` | `application_shell` | transport translation, dependency composition and run lifecycle | source truth, domain state, policy defaults or business decisions | rebuild |
| `M01` | `source_admission` | source identity, admission, raw capture, source time and raw-signal registration | preparation, cognition or capital decisions | replace boundary; retain selected validation kernels |
| `M02` | `calendar` | four calendar layers, fact candidates, coalescence, supersession and projection | candidate formation or capital authority | retain reference algorithms; rebuild state/composition |
| `M03` | `preparation` | typed prepared truth, field-candidate resolution and enrichment gaps | raw-source ownership or serial-stage decisions | rebuild core; retain deterministic transforms |
| `M04` | `packet_protocol` | DMPV3 outer-envelope validation and deliberate packet carriage | raw-ingress carriage, domain decisions, identity or time fabrication | retain envelope shape; replace builders/defaults |
| `M05` | `serial_cognition` | six ordered candidate-forming stages and stage-local carriers | Parallel Risk, capital state or broker execution | retain order/local kernels; rebuild orchestration |
| `M06` | `parallel_risk` | independent environmental risk assessment and terminal-join contribution | candidate formation or capital authority | retain evaluators; rebuild composition/state |
| `M07` | `coefficient_modifier_authority` | versioned coefficient/modifier bundles, precedence and application receipts | calendar, capital, lifecycle, execution or replay policy generally | refactor and narrow |
| `M08` | `playbook_catalogue` | versioned playbook definitions, catalogue and eligibility semantics | runtime capital or execution decisions | refactor behind catalogue contract |
| `M09` | `capital_authority` | final-risk join, capital state, bounded sizing and fresh-capital decision | order placement or position lifecycle | retain calculations; rebuild join/state boundary |
| `M10` | `execution_gateway` | opening readiness, placement intent, execution port and execution events | canonical portfolio state | replace boundary/core adapters |
| `M11` | `portfolio_lifecycle` | fills, positions, inventory, exit/close and carry lifecycle | broker transport or capital admission | retain policy kernels; rebuild state ownership |
| `M12` | `evidence_replay` | review projections, explanation, comparison and authoritative replay | live runtime decision authority | retain read projections; rebuild replay core |
| `M13` | `state_kernel` | transaction mechanism, idempotency, atomic receipts and storage ports | semantic ownership of domain records | rebuild core |
| `M14` | `proof_promotion` | proof manifests, tier decisions, checkpoints and promotion receipts | production-domain decisions | retain/refactor governance plane |
| `M15` | `vocabulary_governance` | durable-term admission, canonical registry and projections | runtime-domain decisions | retain/refactor governance plane |

The module map is many-to-many with the architecture surfaces. A surface is not automatically one package, and one module may serve more than one surface. Physical placement or an `Mxx` label does not prove lawful ownership; accepted inputs, outputs, state and prohibited authority remain decisive.

### 11.10.1 Dependency planes

Every architecture dependency must declare one of these planes:

1. **Runtime/contract dependency** — a production call or consumed public contract.
2. **State/transaction participation** — a domain owner participates in the `M13` transaction mechanism without transferring semantic ownership.
3. **Governance/promotion prerequisite** — `M14` proof or `M15` vocabulary acceptance required before promotion, not a universal production import.
4. **Build/test dependency** — fixtures, proof runners, generators or static checks used only during development and validation.

The accepted runtime/contract graph is:

| Module | Runtime/contract dependencies |
|---|---|
| `M01` | none |
| `M02` | `M01` |
| `M03` | `M01`, `M02` |
| `M04` | none |
| `M05` | `M03`, `M04`, `M07`, `M08` |
| `M06` | `M03`, `M04` |
| `M07` | none |
| `M08` | none |
| `M09` | `M05`, `M06`, `M07` |
| `M10` | `M09` |
| `M11` | `M10` |
| `M12` | `M04` plus read-only evidence-export contracts |
| `M13` | none |

`M00` composes the runtime and is omitted from the domain graph; no production-domain module may depend on `M00`. `M14` and `M15` govern acceptance and promotion and must not be represented as universal runtime imports.

State-owning modules may participate in `M13` transactions while retaining their own records and meaning. The expected alpha participants include `M01`, `M02`, `M06`, `M09`, `M10`, `M11` and `M12` where their accepted scope mutates state and a receipt.

### 11.10.2 Progressive package migration

The target physical namespace is `src/nvda_desk/mxx_module_name/`. Migration is progressive:

- create a package only when its module becomes active;
- move, refactor or replace only the active ownership boundary;
- expose one explicit public package API;
- direct temporary compatibility adapters toward the target owner;
- add no new domain behaviour to superseded locations;
- remove compatibility paths only after consumers migrate and proof passes;
- run dependency and cycle checks after each module.

The module ID belongs at package and architectural boundaries. Local variables, private helpers and non-exported implementation details remain module-local vocabulary and do not require an `Mxx` symbol prefix.

The present source package graph contains two known small cycles, `contracts ↔ packets` and `cognition ↔ domain`. They are implementation defects to eliminate during the relevant module migrations; they do not alter the accepted acyclic target graph.

### 11.10.3 Implementation obligations and sequence

The 33 existing `D-xxx` records remain immutable traceability references. They are not packages, are not automatically branches and do not impose a numeric total order. They are regrouped beneath the active ownership module and may be retained, expanded, split, merged, replaced with lineage preserved, or retired with reason.

Positive module obligations not adequately represented by the historical set are mandatory for:

- `M07` coefficient/modifier bundle, precedence, version and application-receipt authority;
- `M09` capital state, bounded sizing, fresh-capital decision and deployment receipt;
- `M10` alpha execution port, paper adapter, placement intent, order/fill events and execution receipt, with no live-execution authority;
- `M11` fill reconciliation, canonical position/inventory state and atomic lifecycle receipts.

Delivery may assign several dependency-ready modules or bounded module leaves concurrently under the PM-controlled Parallel Workflow after its control checkpoint passes Independent Control Test and PM acceptance. Concurrency is permitted only when semantic ownership, read/write sets and physical locks are non-overlapping. Canonical acceptance and integration remain serial, one approved assignment at a time.

The dependency-wave order remains `M13`; roots `M01`, `M04`, `M07`, `M08`; then `M02`; then `M03`; then independent `M05` and `M06`; then `M09`, `M10`, `M11`; then `M12`; and final `M00` composition. This order is a dependency model, not an instruction to fill unused lanes with downstream work. `M14` and `M15` remain PM-controlled governance planes.


### 11.10.4 Parallel delivery boundary

A Parallel Execution Lane is capacity, not module ownership or a long-lived branch. Each Lane Assignment uses one fresh worker branch and one independent tester thread from a named canonical checkpoint. A Lane Assignment cannot mutate another module's contract, a shared proof or vocabulary registry, root configuration, composition wiring or global project-control state unless the PM opens an exclusive integration unit.

Workers and testers may overlap in time. The PM imports approved results sequentially, reruns affected proof and publishes one combined checkpoint. A tester PASS returns a Tester-approved Portability Artefact suitable for PM integration review; it does not alter canonical architecture, authority or implementation status.

### 11.10.5 Proof relationship

The module architecture does not waive the direction normative authority → truthful proof → implementation. Gate C now applies that sequence locally to the active module. Unrelated unreviewed tests remain visible as `LEGACY_SHADOW_UNADMITTED` and cannot dictate the active implementation merely because they pass or fail. Final whole-system reconciliation remains mandatory before any end-to-end or production-readiness claim.

## External-source lineage for this addendum

- Google Cloud modular-design guidance, reviewed 2026-07-17: supports well-defined independent modules and clear interfaces inside either a monolith or later separated architecture.
- Microsoft design-for-evolution guidance, reviewed 2026-07-17: supports high cohesion, loose coupling, encapsulated domain knowledge and change without ripple through unrelated components.
- IBM module-partitioning guidance, reviewed 2026-07-17: supports packaging by encapsulation boundary, minimising dependencies and assigning stronger ownership.
- OpenAI Agents SDK documentation, reviewed 2026-07-17: confirms that agent runtimes introduce explicit model, tool, handoff, guardrail and tracing concerns; those concerns are rejected from the current deterministic runtime because no agent capability is authorised.

The project adopts modularity, cohesion, loose coupling, implementation hiding, explicit contracts and bounded ownership. It rejects vendor deployment products, microservices by default, cloud infrastructure, enterprise service architecture and agent abstractions where the present deterministic alpha has no corresponding requirement.

## Rules

- Read this addendum with the core; where it is silent, the core governs.
- The five superseded implementation-truth cells are 5.10 `reserved`, 5.11 `placeholder`, 5.12 `placeholder`, 5.13 `placeholder` and 5.14 `reserved`; all are now `thin_seam` for current alpha truth only.
- The stale acceptance-receipt reference cannot be used as current proof.
- Do not upgrade any surface to `implemented` from local code, local tests, receipts or path presence alone.
- Do not freeze a detailed implementation choice merely to make the architecture look complete.
- Do not import production or AI-agent architecture into the deterministic alpha without new user authority and a numbered normative addendum.

