**6. Project-specific parallel execution law**  
  
**6.1 Governing rule**  
  
One Master Project Manager controls canonical state. Multiple bounded assignments may be active only when all of the following are true:  
  
	1.	each assignment is one semantic acceptance unit;  
	2.	each module or behaviour is dependency-ready for the exact leaf being opened;  
	2.	each module or behaviour is dependency-ready for the exact leaf being opened;  
	3.	semantic authority does not overlap or contradict another open assignment;  
	3.	semantic authority does not overlap or contradict another open assignment;  
	4.	physical write sets are disjoint;  
	4.	physical write sets are disjoint;  
	5.	shared contracts have one declared owner;  
	6.	global control, proof and vocabulary surfaces are locked to the PM unless one exclusive control assignment opens them;  
	6.	global control, proof and vocabulary surfaces are locked to the PM unless one exclusive control assignment opens them;  
	7.	each assignment starts from a named immutable canonical checkpoint;  
	7.	each assignment starts from a named immutable canonical checkpoint;  
	8.	worker output is independently reconstructed and tested;  
	8.	worker output is independently reconstructed and tested;  
	9.	tester PASS does not equal canonical acceptance;  
	9.	tester PASS does not equal canonical acceptance;  
	10.	canonical integration remains sequential.  
  
**6.2 Parallelism classes**  
  
|Class                                 |Parallel status                                                                                          |  
|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|  
|Read-only inventory/mechanical routing|Parallel where governed joins and outputs are disjoint                                                   |  
|Authority sufficiency analysis        |Parallel for independent modules; no downstream contract acceptance across unresolved upstream boundaries|  
|Authority sufficiency analysis        |Parallel for independent modules; no downstream contract acceptance across unresolved upstream boundaries|  
|Factual test observation              |Parallel where each physical file has one observation owner or a PM-approved read-only partition         |  
|Factual test observation              |Parallel where each physical file has one observation owner or a PM-approved read-only partition         |  
|Test disposition                      |Parallel by distinct proposition; PM acceptance remains bounded and serial per disposition               |  
|Test disposition                      |Parallel by distinct proposition; PM acceptance remains bounded and serial per disposition               |  
|Proof specification                   |Parallel where contracts and terms are frozen                                                            |  
|Test mutation                         |Parallel only with exclusive test-file ownership and disjoint support files                              |  
|Test mutation                         |Parallel only with exclusive test-file ownership and disjoint support files                              |  
|Production implementation             |Parallel only after proof opens it, runtime prerequisites pass, and exact write sets are disjoint        |  
|Normative/vocabulary mutation         |Serial PM-controlled mutation unless one exclusive lane is expressly opened                              |  
|Normative/vocabulary mutation         |Serial PM-controlled mutation unless one exclusive lane is expressly opened                              |  
|Canonical integration                 |Always serial                                                                                            |  
|Programme-state closeout              |Always PM-controlled                                                                                     |  
|Programme-state closeout              |Always PM-controlled                                                                                     |  
  
**6.3 What a lane is not**  
  
A lane is not:  
  
	●	permanent ownership of a module;  
	●	a branch with authority to continue indefinitely;  
	●	permission to choose follow-on work;  
	●	a place to hold unintegrated dependencies indefinitely;  
	●	a place to hold unintegrated dependencies indefinitely;  
	●	a substitute for the module-local proof sequence;  
	●	a second canonical programme.  
	●	a second canonical programme.  
  
  
  
**7. Mandatory lock model**  
  
Every brief must include a lock manifest with these classes.  
  
**7.1 PM-only global locks**  
  
Unless an explicit exclusive control unit opens them, workers and testers must not mutate:  
  
	●	AGENTS.md  
	●	PLANS.md  
	●	PLANS.md  
	●	ROADMAP_LEDGER.md  
	●	ROADMAP_LEDGER.md  
	●	ROADMAP_LEDGER_V2.md  
	●	ROADMAP_LEDGER_V2.md  
	●	docs/00_CONTROL_SURFACE_ROUTING_INDEX.md  
	●	docs/00_CONTROL_SURFACE_ROUTING_INDEX.md  
	●	numbered normative core/working documents  
	●	project_control/canonical_project_manager/EXEC_PLAN.md  
	●	project_control/canonical_project_manager/GATE_C_COMPLETION_PROTOCOL.md  
	●	project_control/canonical_project_manager/GATE_C_COMPLETION_PROTOCOL.md  
	●	canonical architecture workbook, JSON, graphs and decision report  
	●	config/retained_test_registry.yaml  
	●	config/proof_blocks.yaml  
	●	config/proof_blocks.yaml  
	●	human/generated vocabulary registries  
	●	human/generated vocabulary registries  
	●	root dependency/packaging files and lockfiles  
	●	shared migrations  
	●	shared migrations  
	●	MASTER_WORKFLOW_REGISTRY.md  
  
Workers return proposed control or vocabulary deltas as evidence; the PM decides and integrates them serially.  
  
**7.2 Contract-owner lock**  
  
Each exported cross-module contract has one owning module. Only that module’s authorised assignment may mutate the contract. Consumers adapt later through their own units after the owner checkpoint is canonical.  
  
Examples:  
  
	●	K01–K03: M01  
	●	K07: M04  
	●	K07: M04  
	●	K12: M07  
	●	K13: M08  
	●	K13: M08  
  
**7.3 Physical write lock**  
  
Each assignment must list exact writable files or path prefixes. Any newly discovered required path outside the list causes RETURN_SCOPE_REOPEN_REQUIRED before mutation.  
  
**7.4 Test-file lock**  
  
A physical test file may have only one mutating assignment at a time, even when functions concern different modules. Shared historical Gate C files may be observed by several lanes, but only one lane may mutate them.  
  
New module-local proof should normally be written in a module-owned proof path rather than making concurrent edits to shared legacy test files.  
  
**7.5 Read-set/staleness lock**  
  
Each assignment records the authoritative documents, contracts, tests and source surfaces it relied on. If canonical integration changes any material item in that read set before the assignment is integrated, the tester verdict becomes STALE_REVALIDATION_REQUIRED.  
  
  
  
**8. Assignment unit and lifecycle**  
  
**8.1 Unit size**  
  
One assignment is one MLC leaf or one bounded implementation slice. It is not “complete M01” or “fix all packet code”.  
  
Standard module-local sequence:  
  
MLC-A → MLC-R → MLC-O → MLC-D → MLC-P → MLC-T → MLC-V → MLC-I  
  
A leaf may be split further only where the semantic propositions are independently owned, proven and integrated.  
  
**8.2 Project-specific lifecycle**  
  
ALLOCATED → WORKER_ACTIVE → CANDIDATE_RETURNED → TESTER_ACTIVE → TESTER_PASS/FAIL_REPAIRABLE/HARD_BLOCK → PM_ACCEPTED_FOR_INTEGRATION → QUEUED → INTEGRATED → AGGREGATE_VALIDATED → CLOSED  
  
Additional required states:  
  
	●	RETURN_SCOPE_REOPEN_REQUIRED  
	●	BLOCKED_MISSING_AUTHORITY  
	●	BLOCKED_MISSING_AUTHORITY  
	●	BLOCKED_DEPENDENCY  
	●	STALE_REVALIDATION_REQUIRED  
	●	STALE_REVALIDATION_REQUIRED  
	●	INTEGRATION_CONFLICT_REPAIR_REQUIRED  
  
**8.3 Repairs**  
  
A repair remains with the same worker/tester assignment while the original semantic scope is unchanged. Any repair that changes authority, contract ownership, dependencies, write set or architecture returns to the PM for a new assignment or scope amendment.  
  
After repeated false-positive repairs or a material architecture defect, the PM must perform an independent falsification review rather than allowing the pair to optimise indefinitely against its own test family.  
  
  
  
